on the real reason  that we're pursuing this is because it's  potentially putting a ceiling on our  market a ceiling on our business a  ceiling on how many boards we can build  a ceiling on how many  square-feet that we can rant how many  people we can hire I've learned that  very few things are easy in the  legislative process especially when it  involves change and new technologies and  so a lot of the challenge with being  able to move a bill like this through  the legislature is dispelling the myths  that are in people's minds that really  have nothing to do with what this board  is about just ask fella foremen who took  valuable time away from his five-man  business operation to trek up to  Sacramento and educate lawmakers so we  had an electric skateboard demonstration  in a Plaza outside the capital last year  my partner went around different offices  at the Capitol to get people  demonstrations of the product informant  says reviews were positive their  proposed legislation didn't meet any  serious opposition aside from some  cyclists who expressed concern about  having to share the road electric  skateboarding in a lot of ways it isn't  a partisan issue  it's clean it's creating jobs but even  without any political opposition EB 2054  has had to go back to the drafting stage  because as Foreman found out the  legislative process is extremely  convoluted if it had been me personally  all by myself trying to figure out the  future this legislation I would have had  no clue where to start we discovered as  we were working on 80/20 54 in the  legislative process that there are a lot  of technical challenges that need to be  fixed to make sure that it's consistent  with other parts of the vehicle code and  the language is being written concisely  enough Olsen plans to reintroduce the  bill early next year and despite the  snail's pace of the political process  they are still optimistic about reform  so it's been an uphill battle the fact  that it's taking a litt